<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">V. P. Menon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1893">1893</a>–<a href="/years/1966">1966</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">V. P. Menon was an Indian civil servant who, as secretary of the States Ministry under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vallabhbhai-patel">Vallabhbhai Patel</a>, oversaw the integration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> after 1947. He drafted the Instrument of Accession and negotiated with rulers to bring more than five hundred states into the new union. He had earlier been constitutional adviser to the last viceroys and helped shape the partition plan of 1947.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Vappala Pangunni Menon was born on 30 September 1893 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>, the son of a school headmaster, Chunangad Shankara Menon. He dropped out of formal education, never earning a high school diploma or a college degree, and worked as a railway stoker, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> miner and a clerk for a tobacco company in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengaluru">Bangalore</a>. He then joined the Imperial Secretariat Service, becoming a superintendent in the Reforms Office in 1930 and rising to joint secretary to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india">Government of India</a> by 1941. Encouraged by Edwin Montagu, he became the highest serving Indian officer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Menon was joint secretary to the Simla Conference in June 1945 and in 1946 became Political Reforms Commissioner to the Viceroy. As political adviser to Lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-mountbatten">Louis Mountbatten</a>, he proposed to Mountbatten, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vallabhbhai-patel">Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">Muslim League</a>'s plan to partition <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> into two independent nations, India and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, after the interim government collapsed. Under Patel he became the envoy to the princes. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanwant-singh">Hanwant Singh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a>, pointed a pistol at him after signing his accession, Menon told him he would be making a very serious mistake.</p><p>On 17 September 1947 he delivered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s protest against Junagadh's accession to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> to its dewan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-nawaz-bhutto">Sir Shah Nawaz Bhutto</a>. He drafted and negotiated the Standstill Agreement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a>, and on 26 October flew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a> to secure the signature of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hari-singh">Maharaja Hari Singh</a> on the accession of Jammu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>. In May 1948 a meeting he initiated led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajpramukh">Rajpramukhs</a> of the princely unions to sign new Instruments of Accession, and he signed the Tripura Merger Agreement for the Governor-General.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After his time as secretary, Menon was briefly Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, then called Orissa, in 1951. He wrote The Story of the Integration of Indian States and Transfer of Power, an account of the partition. He later joined the free-market Swatantra Party but never contested elections. He had been made Rao Bahadur, CIE in 1941 and CSI in 1946, but in 1948 he refused a knighthood, saying that with independence he had entered the service of the new Indian government; according to his grandson, he privately said he could not accept it for having caused the partition of his country.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “V. P. Menon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Witnesses and historians hold that the riots did not sway the ruler; after negotiations with <b>V. P. Menon</b>, Chithira Thirunal agreed to accede to the Indian Union on 12 August 1947.</blockquote><a href="/empire/travancore">Travancore · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Their rulers had signed Instruments of Accession surrendering defence, foreign affairs and communications to the Dominion of India, but the States Ministry under Vallabhbhai Patel, with its secretary V.P. <b>Menon</b>, judged most of them too small to survive as separate units and pressed them to merge.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-state-of-rajasthan">United State of Rajasthan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>With British paramountcy at an end, Patel and <b>V. P. Menon</b> persuaded almost all the princes whose states lay within India to accede to it before 15 August, while the states within Pakistan’s borders joined Pakistan.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>Vallabhbhai Patel and <b>V. P. Menon</b> integrated more than five hundred princely states, using force against Hyderabad in 1948.</blockquote><a href="/state/dominion-of-india">Dominion of India</a></li><li><blockquote>In early 1948, under pressure from <b>V. P. Menon</b> and Sardar Patel, the rulers agreed to merge their states into the United State of Kathiawar, inaugurated in February 1948; Junagadh State joined in 1949.</blockquote><a href="/state/saurashtra-state">Saurashtra State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ottapalam, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jabalpur">Jabalpur</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=10.77,76.38&amp;year=1940" aria-label="Ottapalam on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of V. P. Menon"><image href="/maps/globes/g09000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3866 4084h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3866 4084h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=10.77,76.38&amp;year=1940">What was here in 1940? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
